cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use this project, please cite it as below and include the commit hash you used.
type: software
title: "Péterfalvi's Problem for p = 3: No Group Satisfies Hypothesis (B)"
authors:
  - family-names: Ishida
    given-names: Yawara
    email: yawara@aisr.dev
    affiliation: "A.I.System Research, Inc."
repository-code: "https://github.com/yawara/peterfalvi-problem"
url: "https://github.com/yawara/peterfalvi-problem"
license: Apache-2.0
abstract: >-
  A Lean 4 proof that, for p = 3 and every prime q with q ∤ p − 1, no group
  satisfies hypothesis (B) of Proposition 9 of Glauberman and Norton (Proc.
  Amer. Math. Soc. 119, 1993). This answers Péterfalvi's question at the end of
  that paper, which is also Problem 1 in Appendix C of Bender and Glauberman,
  Local Analysis for the Odd Order Theorem (1994). The repository also proves
  Péterfalvi's example: SL(2, 2^q) satisfies the hypothesis for p = 2.
keywords:
  - Lean 4
  - mathlib
  - formalization
  - odd order theorem
  - finite group theory
  - finite fields
references:
  - type: article
    title: On a combinatorial problem associated with the odd order theorem
    authors:
      - family-names: Glauberman
        given-names: George
      - family-names: Norton
        given-names: Simon P.
    journal: Proceedings of the American Mathematical Society
    volume: 119
    issue: 4
    start: 1089
    end: 1094
    year: 1993
    doi: 10.1090/S0002-9939-1993-1160299-X
  - type: book
    title: Local Analysis for the Odd Order Theorem
    authors:
      - family-names: Bender
        given-names: Helmut
      - family-names: Glauberman
        given-names: George
    collection-title: London Mathematical Society Lecture Note Series
    volume: 188
    publisher:
      name: Cambridge University Press
    year: 1994
    isbn: 0-521-45716-5
